/-
# Routed-prefix ceilings for symmetric old-to-new BF4 flow

A carrier-wide one-edge Harnack ceiling is stronger than the symmetric cross
argument actually consumes.  Each nonzero summand uses only one constructive
prefix of one canonical path: the forward term stops at the selected edge
source, and the reverse term continues through that edge to its target.

This module packages bounds only for those two families of routed products.
It proves that a global one-edge ceiling implies the routed datum, but the
compiler itself accepts the strictly weaker routed hypotheses.  Their two
finite ceiling loads still bound the exact symmetric cross transition-product
load and compile to the old-to-new conditional capacity certificate.

No routed ceiling or payment is inhabited here.  No uniform capacity estimate,
thermodynamic/continuum construction, or Clay conclusion is asserted.

No `Classical.*`, choice, `native_decide`, `sorry`, custom axiom, or
noncomputable declaration is used.
-/

